# =============================================================================
# Omnitech Studio: environment reference
# =============================================================================
#
# HOW TO USE THIS FILE
#   cp .env.example .env     (then edit .env; it is ignored by Git)
#   pnpm dev
#
# WHAT YOU MUST PROVIDE BEFORE THE FIRST RUN
#   1. A language model: ONE of the four options in section 1. With none set,
#      `pnpm dev` falls back to LM Studio, which only works when LM Studio is
#      installed with its local server running. Generated answers, the
#      assistant and Active Session need a model; the rest of the app starts
#      without one.
#   2. Docker running (PostgreSQL comes from compose.yaml, and code runs in
#      Docker). Build the code-runner images once: `pnpm runner:build`.
# Everything else has a working local default supplied by `pnpm dev`: sign-in,
# secrets, the database URL, the tenant and owner. Every line below is commented
# out except the two tooling defaults in section 14, so an unedited copy changes
# nothing. Uncomment a line to change its default.
#
# WHO READS WHAT
#   pnpm dev       loads the root .env, then apps/web/.env.local (which wins).
#                  A variable exported in your shell wins over both.
#   direct runs    `pnpm --filter <package> <script>`, a worker started by
#                  hand, Playwright and the CLI do NOT load .env: export the
#                  variable in your shell for those.
#   `next dev`     run by itself reads apps/web/.env*, not the root .env.
# Both .env and apps/web/.env.local are ignored by Git. Never commit secrets.
#
# `scripts/env-docs.test.ts` fails when the code reads a variable that is not
# documented here (or in the README) or this file names one that nothing reads.
#
# CONTENTS
#    1. Language model for generated answers and Active Session   <- start here
#    2. Studio assistant through Claude Code
#    3. Agent worker and coding agents (Codex, Claude Code)
#    4. Active Session
#    5. Database and secrets
#    6. Sign-in and connected accounts
#    7. Interview data and the CLI
#    8. Image generation
#    9. Terminal gateway
#   10. Document generation limits
#   11. On-device (WebGPU) model
#   12. Build and the dev launcher
#   13. Maintainer scripts
#   14. Tooling defaults (documentation and browser tests)


# =============================================================================
# 1. Language model for generated answers and Active Session   (YOU CHOOSE)
# =============================================================================
# Pick ONE option below. When several are set, the order is:
#   1) AI_BASE_URL + AI_MODEL   2) OpenAI (OPENAI_MODEL or OPENAI_API_KEY)
#   3) LM Studio (LM_STUDIO_MODEL)
# Every configured provider is offered in the picker; AI_DEFAULT_PROVIDER_ID
# chooses which one is used when a caller names none (openai or lm-studio).
#
# If NONE of AI_MODEL, OPENAI_MODEL, OPENAI_API_KEY and LM_STUDIO_MODEL is set,
# `pnpm dev` uses the model LM Studio already has loaded, or asks LM Studio for
# qwen/qwen3-coder-30b, and says which on start. Do not set AI_MODEL just to
# "fill it in": setting any of the four turns that fallback off.
#
# --- Option A: LM Studio on this machine (no key needed) --------------------
# Start LM Studio's local server (Developer tab), then either leave everything
# below unset, or name the model you want. `pnpm dev` loads it with a 65,536
# token window so the assistant's history fits (section 12).
# LM_STUDIO_MODEL=qwen/qwen3-coder-30b
# LM_STUDIO_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1
# LM_STUDIO_API_KEY=
# LM_STUDIO_LOCALITY=device
#
# --- Option B: OpenAI -------------------------------------------------------
# A key alone selects the default hosted model (gpt-5-mini).
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5-mini
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
# OPENAI_LOCALITY=remote
#
# --- Option C: any other OpenAI-compatible /v1 endpoint ---------------------
# (Ollama's /v1 proxy, vLLM, Together, a company gateway ...). AI_BASE_URL and
# AI_MODEL are required together. A localhost URL is labelled "LM Studio" unless
# you name it with AI_PROVIDER_ID and AI_PROVIDER_LABEL.
# AI_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1
# AI_MODEL=llama3.1:8b
# AI_API_KEY=
# AI_PROVIDER_ID=ollama
# AI_PROVIDER_LABEL=Ollama
# AI_LOCALITY=device
#
# --- Option D: deterministic fake model (tests and demos, not real answers) --
# AI_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/fake/v1
# AI_MODEL=fake-interview-model
#
# --- Shared settings --------------------------------------------------------
# Which configured provider is the default when several are set.
# AI_DEFAULT_PROVIDER_ID=openai
# Request timeout in ms. Default 120000; `pnpm dev` raises it to 600000 for a
# local model, which can take minutes on a long briefing.
# AI_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
# Declared locality of each model: device, private-network or remote (default).
# `device` counts only when the base URL is loopback (localhost, 127.0.0.1,
# ::1); it is never inferred, and a non-loopback URL downgrades it to remote.
# A device-only Active Session may use only `device` models.
# AI_LOCALITY=remote
# Optional Anthropic API model for platform AI targets (default model
# claude-sonnet-5-5). Unset disables the target.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5
# The record of every AI interaction (@omnitech/ai-engine). With
# AI_ENGINE_DATABASE_URL set, the web app writes one row per model call, image
# and repair turn to the engine's own tables in that database. Unset: no record
# is written. The engine keeps its tables in a schema named "ai", and Studio's
# own database already has an "ai" schema with different tables, so this must
# name a separate database, where the engine's tables were created with its own
# setup (`ai-engine db setup --url <this url>` in the omnitech-ai-engine
# repository). A database without them loses the records and no call fails.
# AI_ENGINE_CAPTURE=full also keeps prompts and outputs with each row; any
# other value, or none, keeps ids, timing, usage and outcome only. Leave it
# unset wherever content must not be stored.
# AI_ENGINE_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://omnitech:omnitech@127.0.0.1:54320/omnitech_ai_engine
# AI_ENGINE_CAPTURE=full
# AI_ENGINE_LOG_LEVEL: how much the AI engine says in the server's and the
# worker's own log, through the Studio's logger (events named `ai.*`): trace,
# debug, info, warn, error, or silent. Unset, it follows NODE_ENV: everything
# ("trace") in development, nothing ("silent") in production and in a test run.
#   info:  one line per call, job and conversation, with what it was for, who
#          answered, how long, tokens and cost; warnings (a retry, a fallback,
#          a refusal) and errors.
#   debug: also each attempt, each guard that stopped a call, provider status.
#   trace: also the whole prompt and the whole answer of every call
#          (`ai.prompt`, `ai.completion`), but ONLY where LOG_CONTENT allows
#          content (below). `pnpm dev` allows it; production does not unless
#          LOG_CONTENT=true and LOG_LEVEL=trace are both set on purpose.
# A credential is never written, in any mode.
# AI_ENGINE_LOG_LEVEL=info


# =============================================================================
# 2. Studio assistant through Claude Code
# =============================================================================
# Make the docked assistant default to your signed-in Claude Code CLI. This does
# NOT configure the model in section 1, which generated answers and Active
# Session always use. A model the person picked in the browser wins until
# changed in the assistant picker; if this target is unavailable it falls back.
# INTERVIEW_ASSISTANT_DEFAULT_MODEL=agent/claude-code
# CLAUDE_ASSISTANT_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5


# =============================================================================
# 3. Agent worker and coding agents (Codex, Claude Code)
# =============================================================================
# apps/agent-worker runs Codex and Claude Code jobs and the Active Session loop;
# `pnpm dev` starts it. Defaults are the model ids in
# packages/ai-runtime/src/config.ts.
# CLAUDE_DOCUMENT_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5
# CLAUDE_FALLBACK_MODEL=
# CODEX_FAST_MODEL=gpt-5.3-codex
# CODEX_QUALITY_MODEL=gpt-5.3-codex
# CODEX_ASSISTANT_MODEL=gpt-5.3-codex
# Codex CLI binary (default: `codex` found on PATH, else the SDK's bundled one).
# CODEX_PATH=
# Worker tuning: its name (a unique id is added per start), jobs at once (1-16;
# keep above DOCUMENTS_MAX_PARALLEL_CALLS), lease and idle poll in ms.
# AGENT_WORKER_ID=worker
# AGENT_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=6
# AGENT_WORKER_LEASE_MS=30000
# AGENT_WORKER_POLL_MS=100


# =============================================================================
# 4. Active Session (read by apps/agent-worker, which must be running)
# =============================================================================
# No model in section 1 means the session loop is off. Studio never submits or
# operates an external interview interface for you.
# `docker` lets the coding path run tests in the sandboxed Docker runner (build
# it once with `pnpm runner:build`); unset means no tests ever run.
# ACTIVE_SESSION_CODE_RUNNER=docker
# The Docker stack's web app cannot reach Docker: it calls the host's code runner
# service (packages/code-runner/src/host-main.ts) at CODE_RUNNER_URL with CODE_RUNNER_TOKEN
# (24+ characters; compose and the host script share a local default).
# CODE_RUNNER_URL=http://host.docker.internal:3002
# CODE_RUNNER_TOKEN=
# The host runner's port (default 3002, loopback only).
# CODE_RUNNER_PORT=3002
# true declares the Docker runner runs on this device, so a device-only session
# may run tests.
# ACTIVE_SESSION_RUNNER_DEVICE_LOCAL=true
# on lets a validated coding task escalate to a bounded agent job.
# ACTIVE_SESSION_AGENT_ESCALATION=on
#
# The tool-less session agent profiles. `on` serves them through the worker;
# anything else keeps them off. PROFILE pins which runtime answers (claude or
# codex). MAX_TURNS bounds a session answer's turns (integer, at least 6,
# default 6). STAGING_DIR is the private directory attachments are staged in
# (default: a per-process folder under the system temp directory).
# `pnpm dev` and `pnpm app:up` turn the port on and pin `claude` by default, so
# Claude Code serves sessions and screenshot analysis with no LM Studio or API
# endpoint configured. Set these only to change that.
# ACTIVE_SESSION_AGENT_PORT=on
# ACTIVE_SESSION_AGENT_PROFILE=claude
# ACTIVE_SESSION_AGENT_MAX_TURNS=6
# ACTIVE_SESSION_AGENT_STAGING_DIR=
#
# `on` lets a live session's audio sources tell the coach who is speaking right
# now (the call's audio is the interviewer, the microphone is you), so the
# coach waits while the interviewer is still mid-sentence. Detected in the Mac
# app from loudness alone; only "speaking" or "stopped" is sent, never audio,
# and never for a device-only session. Unset or anything else: off, nothing is
# sent and the coach waits out pauses as before.
# Also in Settings (the Mac app's Settings window, "Behaviour"): set here, the
# environment wins and Settings shows it read-only; unset, Settings decides and
# a change applies to the next report with no restart.
# ACTIVE_SESSION_VOICE_ACTIVITY=on
#
# The live coach (agent worker): it reads what a live session hears (or a
# transcript you attach with `node scripts/coach-transcript.mjs`) and writes
# the coach's notes as the conversation goes, on Claude Code (`claude`) or
# Codex (`codex`). Unset or `off`: no coach. It needs INTERVIEW_API_TOKEN and
# reaches Studio at INTERVIEW_API_URL. A device-only session is never read.
# `pnpm dev` turns it on with `claude`.
# Also in Settings ("Behaviour"): set here, the environment wins and Settings
# shows it read-only; unset, Settings decides and the worker follows a change
# within a few seconds, no restart. INTERVIEW_COACH_DEFAULT is a launcher's
# starting value (claude, codex or off) used only while neither this variable
# nor Settings says anything.
# INTERVIEW_COACH=claude
# INTERVIEW_COACH_DEFAULT=claude
# The coach keeps one session of its runtime open for the call, so the model
# remembers the conversation and each turn sends only what is new. `off` puts
# every call to a model that starts from nothing. Also in Settings
# ("Behaviour"), with the same rule: the environment wins when set.
# INTERVIEW_COACH_RETAIN=on


# =============================================================================
# 5. Database and secrets
# =============================================================================
# `pnpm dev` starts PostgreSQL from compose.yaml (loopback port 54320), applies
# the roles step, migrates and bootstraps it, and sets DATABASE_URL and
# DATABASE_OWNER_URL ITSELF: values here are ignored by `pnpm dev`. Set them
# only for direct runs (export them in your shell), for example
# `pnpm --filter @omnitech/database db:migrate`, or a worker started by hand.
#
# Two roles (ADR-0005): the app connects as the RUNTIME role (DATABASE_URL), which
# has DML grants only and cannot disable row-level security or run DDL. Migrations
# connect as the OWNER role (DATABASE_OWNER_URL), which owns the schemas. A
# database with one role (no DATABASE_OWNER_URL) migrates as DATABASE_URL.
# docker/postgres/ensure-roles.sql creates both roles and is safe to run again.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://omnitech:omnitech@127.0.0.1:54320/omnitech
# DATABASE_OWNER_URL=postgresql://omnitech_owner:omnitech_owner@127.0.0.1:54320/omnitech
#
# Secrets. `pnpm dev` supplies throwaway local values for AUTH_SECRET and
# AGENT_PAYLOAD_SECRET; anything shared or deployed MUST set its own. Generate
# one with:  openssl rand -base64 32
# AUTH_SECRET=
# The server refuses to start without one. The only exception is the local
# passwordless sign-in (FAKE_AUTH_ENABLED=true, outside production), which uses
# a fixed development secret: never deploy that.
# At least 32 characters; encrypts short-lived agent inputs, shared by the web
# app and the worker (falls back to CONNECTED_ACCOUNT_SECRET).
# AGENT_PAYLOAD_SECRET=
# Internal token shared by the platform and the terminal gateway; required
# outside local development.
# AGENT_SERVICE_TOKEN=


# =============================================================================
# 6. Sign-in and connected accounts
# =============================================================================
# Local development: `pnpm dev` turns the passwordless "Local development"
# sign-in on by itself (FAKE_AUTH_ENABLED=true). NEVER enable it on a shared or
# deployed instance. Under `next dev` it also skips sign-in altogether (requests
# are the local owner); a production build (`next start`) has no such shortcut,
# so the browser and the macOS app sign in through the local provider instead.
# FAKE_AUTH_ENABLED=true
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FAKE_AUTH_ENABLED=true
#
# Whether Auth.js builds callback URLs from the request's Host header. On by
# default only for the local setup (`next dev`, or FAKE_AUTH_ENABLED=true on
# 127.0.0.1). A hosted production server behind a proxy that sets the Host header
# must set AUTH_TRUST_HOST=true; AUTH_TRUST_HOST=false turns it off anywhere.
# AUTH_TRUST_HOST=true
#
# HOSTING BEHIND A TLS-TERMINATING PROXY: set AUTH_URL to the public https origin
# (for example https://studio.example.com). The session cookie is then Secure and
# named __Secure-authjs.session-token, which is the name Auth.js reads back over
# https; without AUTH_URL the proxy must send X-Forwarded-Proto: https, or the
# macOS app's sign-in hand-off sets a plain cookie that is never found and the
# person is silently signed out. NEXTAUTH_URL is the older name for the same
# setting. Also: set AUTH_SECRET (the server refuses to start without one), set
# AUTH_TRUST_HOST=true, never set FAKE_AUTH_ENABLED, and give the process a hard-
# kill grace period (an in-flight request that never ends keeps it alive).
# AUTH_URL=
# NEXTAUTH_URL=
#
# DOCKER: the compose stack publishes every port on 127.0.0.1 only, but inside the
# container each client arrives from the Docker bridge gateway, which Next reports
# as X-Forwarded-For and the local sign-in reads as "forwarded from elsewhere".
# AUTH_ASSUME_LOOPBACK_CLIENTS=true (set by compose.yaml, not for a real reverse
# proxy) says every client is on this machine; it ignores only that client address,
# never a non-loopback Host or a public X-Forwarded-Host. Exactly `true` counts.
# AUTH_ASSUME_LOOPBACK_CLIENTS=true
#
# Real login providers (Auth.js). Login grants are never reused by integrations.
# AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=
# AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=
# AUTH_LINKEDIN_ID=
# AUTH_LINKEDIN_SECRET=
#
# Connected accounts: SEPARATE OAuth clients for user-authorised product
# actions. Tokens are stored encrypted and never sent to the browser.
# INTEGRATION_STATE_SECRET=
# CONNECTED_ACCOUNT_SECRET=
# INTEGRATION_GOOGLE_ID=
# INTEGRATION_GOOGLE_SECRET=
# INTEGRATION_LINKEDIN_ID=
# INTEGRATION_LINKEDIN_SECRET=
# UNVERIFIED: opt LinkedIn into S256 PKCE with 1 (Google always uses PKCE).
# LinkedIn documents PKCE for native apps; web-client support is unconfirmed.
# INTEGRATION_LINKEDIN_PKCE=0
#
# First tenant and owner that `pnpm dev` and the bootstrap script seed. The
# local app is at /t/<tenant>/p/interview, by default /t/local/p/interview.
# PLATFORM_BOOTSTRAP_EMAIL=local@omnitech.test
# PLATFORM_BOOTSTRAP_TENANT=local
# PLATFORM_BOOTSTRAP_TENANT_NAME=Local Workspace


# =============================================================================
# 7. Interview data and the CLI
# =============================================================================
# Where the JSON answer repository and the library source records live.
# INTERVIEW_DATA_DIR=.data
#
# Bearer token for the /api/v1 API from the CLI and scripts. The signed-in web
# app does not need it (its session cookie is verified instead). Unset, the
# bearer path is closed: a CLI call without a token is refused (401) unless the
# host's local sign-in bypass lets it name the `local` tenant (`--tenant local`).
# INTERVIEW_API_TOKEN=change-me
# Used by CLI clients (`interview-answers configure` can store these instead).
# INTERVIEW_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000
#
# Local seeds for the owner's profile and document templates (outside
# production only). Defaults are the studio folder's own data. The experience
# matrix is the candidate evidence behind experience-based explanations.
# INTERVIEW_DEFAULT_MATRIX_PATH=
# INTERVIEW_EXPERIENCE_MATRIX_PATH=
# INTERVIEW_TEMPLATES_DIR=
#
# The person's contact details for documents (email, phone, portfolio): a JSON
# file kept on this machine and never in git or the experience matrix, e.g.
# {"email": "...", "phone": "...", "portfolio": "..."}. Read outside
# production only, for the local member, into the document's contact fields;
# never logged and never sent to a model. Unset, the first of these that
# exists is read: profile/contact.json under INTERVIEW_DATA_DIR (when that is
# set), then .dev-local/profile/contact.json of the checkout.
# INTERVIEW_CONTACT_PATH=


# =============================================================================
# 8. Image generation
# =============================================================================
# Each provider is used only when its key (or, for ComfyUI, its server) exists.
# OPENAI_API_KEY (section 1) also enables OpenAI images.
# OPENAI_IMAGE_MODEL=gpt-image-1
# TOGETHER_AI_API_KEY=
# TOGETHER_IMAGE_MODEL=black-forest-labs/FLUX.1-schnell-Free
# FAL_API_KEY=
# FAL_IMAGE_MODEL=fal-ai/flux-pro/v1.1-ultra
# COMFYUI_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8188
# COMFYUI_IMAGE_MODEL=z_image_turbo_bf16.safetensors
# JSON workflow submitted to ComfyUI; use {{prompt}} where text is injected.
# COMFYUI_WORKFLOW_JSON=


# =============================================================================
# 9. Terminal gateway (apps/terminal-gateway)
# =============================================================================
# Port, shared token, the web origins it accepts (comma separated; unset allows
# none) and the platform origin it calls.
# TERMINAL_GATEWAY_PORT=3001
# TERMINAL_GATEWAY_TOKEN=
# TERMINAL_GATEWAY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://127.0.0.1:3000
# PLATFORM_HTTP_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000


# =============================================================================
# 10. Document generation limits (read by the web server)
# =============================================================================
# Default and allowed range. An out-of-range value stops startup and names the
# variable.
# DOCUMENTS_MAX_PARALLEL_CALLS=4
# DOCUMENTS_FIELDS_PER_CALL=24
# DOCUMENTS_CALL_ATTEMPTS=2
# DOCUMENTS_FIELD_WORDS=25
# DOCUMENTS_LIST_ITEM_WORDS=15
# DOCUMENTS_SUMMARY_WORDS=60
# DOCUMENTS_SKILL_ITEMS=12
# Ranges: parallel calls 1-8, fields per call 5-100, attempts 1-3, field words
# 5-200, list item words 3-100, summary words 10-300, skill items 3-40.


# =============================================================================
# 11. On-device (WebGPU) model
# =============================================================================
# Optional. ON_DEVICE_MODEL_DIR serves the packed files at /model (unset
# answers 404; `pnpm dev` finds a sibling omnitech-on-device-llm checkout's
# packed model by itself). The browser checks every file against the pinned
# manifest digest; the URL defaults to /model/manifest.json.
# ON_DEVICE_MODEL_DIR=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ON_DEVICE_MODEL_SHA256=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ON_DEVICE_MODEL_URL=/model/manifest.json


# =============================================================================
# 12. Build and the dev launcher
# =============================================================================
# Where Next writes its output (default .next), and the build id shown in the
# app. The id is set by next.config.ts from the git commit; do not set it.
# NEXT_DIST_DIR=.next
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BUILD_ID=
#
# LM Studio load settings used by `pnpm dev` when it loads your model: the
# context window in tokens (default 65536) and the idle TTL in seconds (default
# 14400). DEV_SKIP_LM_STUDIO_LOAD=1 leaves LM Studio alone and lets it load the
# model on first use.
# ASSISTANT_CONTEXT_TOKENS=65536
# ASSISTANT_MODEL_TTL_SECONDS=14400
# DEV_SKIP_LM_STUDIO_LOAD=1
#
# `pnpm dev` also brings the Mac app (Interview Studio) up to date with its
# Swift sources, beside the servers and without waiting for it; it skips when no
# source changed and never restarts a running app. "off" turns that off for the
# run (`pnpm dev:native` still builds by hand).
# DEV_NATIVE_BUILD=off


# =============================================================================
# 13. Maintainer scripts
# =============================================================================
# OMNITECH_ASSISTANT_SRC points scripts/assistant-sync.mjs at the
# omnitech-assistant checkout (default: a sibling directory). BENCHMARK_LIVE=1
# allows the live-provider benchmarks under packages/agent-runtime-*/scripts and
# scripts/benchmark-document-groups.ts, which may use BENCHMARK_CLAUDE_MODEL
# (default sonnet) and BENCHMARK_CODEX_MODEL (default gpt-6-luna).
# OMNITECH_ASSISTANT_SRC=
# BENCHMARK_LIVE=1
# BENCHMARK_CLAUDE_MODEL=sonnet
# BENCHMARK_CODEX_MODEL=gpt-6-luna
#
# Real-provider integration check (`pnpm test:integration`): names the agent
# runtime signed in on this machine, claude-code or codex. Unset skips it, and
# the script refuses to run without it. It sends a fixed, non-private prompt.
# ACTIVE_SESSION_AGENT_INTEGRATION=claude-code


# =============================================================================
# 14. Tooling defaults (documentation and browser tests)
# =============================================================================
# These two lines are ON by default.
#
# Documentation (Crux). derive-arch ignores a repository's own arch extractor
# unless this is 1, and then regenerates bionic/arch/data-model.md as a stub,
# which shows as false drift. This repository's extractor is
# tools/crux/arch/drizzle_data_model.py (wired in .bionic.yml). Only
# `pnpm docs:arch` and `pnpm docs:arch:check` read it from here or the
# environment (default 1); Crux's own scripts do not read .env. For a raw
# derive-arch, audit-docs or check-drift run, prefix that one command with
# CRUX_ARCH_ALLOW_OVERRIDES=1 and never export it in a shell profile: it lets
# Crux run repository Python, so a profile export does that in every repository.
CRUX_ARCH_ALLOW_OVERRIDES=1
# CRUX_PLUGIN_ROOT is the installed Crux plugin directory (default: the newest
# version under ~/.claude/plugins/cache/crux/crux).
# CRUX_PLUGIN_ROOT=
#
# Browser end-to-end suite (`pnpm test:browser`, e2e/live-session; never part of
# `pnpm verify`). It needs Docker and the Playwright browsers
# (`pnpm test:browser:install`). E2E_SHARDS is how many parallel shards run the
# whole suite. Unset, it follows the machine: one shard per two CPU cores, from 1
# to 4 (a 2-core CI runner runs one process). Set it to force a count (at most
# 8; 1 runs one process). Each shard has its own stack: PostgreSQL container,
# ports, worker and storage state. Naming spec files, --shard, --list, --ui or
# --debug runs one process.
# E2E_SHARDS=4
# E2E_SHARD is set per shard by the runner; never set it. E2E_STACK_CONFIG is
# set by the suite's global setup for its workers; never set it.
# Optional: E2E_HEADED=1 shows the browser; E2E_STRICT=1 makes claims still
# marked `pending` in e2e/live-session/src/claims/claims.ts fail the run;
# E2E_REBUILD=1 forces a fresh `next build` (otherwise the build is reused while
# the source is unchanged); E2E_BROWSER_CHANNEL=chrome runs the Chromium project
# in the installed Google Chrome instead of Playwright's Chromium; E2E_LIVE=1
# prints a wall-clock START/END line per test (project, file:line, title);
# E2E_DIST_DIR names the web build directory (default .next-e2e; it must start
# with .next-e2e and hold no path separator) so parallel runs do not share one.
# E2E_HEADED=1
# E2E_STRICT=1
# E2E_REBUILD=1
# E2E_BROWSER_CHANNEL=chrome
# E2E_LIVE=1
# E2E_DIST_DIR=.next-e2e
# E2E_SHARD=
# E2E_STACK_CONFIG=
# E2E_WALKTHROUGH=1 registers the QA walkthrough spec (e2e/live-session/tests/walkthrough.spec.ts: every
# screen and native-panel state, dark and light, with screenshots and a findings file); E2E_WALKTHROUGH_DIR
# names where it writes them (default /tmp/walkthrough). Never set in verify or CI.
# E2E_WALKTHROUGH=1
# E2E_WALKTHROUGH_DIR=/tmp/walkthrough

# Swift lint (scripts/swiftlint.mjs): SwiftLint needs sourcekitd. On a machine with only the
# Command Line Tools it cannot find it, so the script sets TOOLCHAIN_DIR to the active developer
# directory when this is unset. Set it yourself only to point SwiftLint at another toolchain.
# TOOLCHAIN_DIR=/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools

# Build tag in the session footer (dev builds only). apps/web/next.config.ts bakes the git short
# SHA and branch into the page at build time (NEXT_PUBLIC_BUILD_SHA, NEXT_PUBLIC_BUILD_BRANCH); they
# are derived, never set by hand. Set BUILD_PACKAGED=1 when building the packaged app so the tag is
# hidden (the build then bakes NEXT_PUBLIC_BUILD_PACKAGED=1); unset or any other value shows the tag.
# BUILD_PACKAGED=1
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BUILD_SHA=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BUILD_BRANCH=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BUILD_PACKAGED=

# Logging (packages/logging, used by the web app's live-session routes and the AI gateway).
# LOG_LEVEL: error | warn | info | debug | trace (default info in production, warn in test, debug otherwise).
# LOG_FORMAT: json | pretty | story (default json in production). "story" is what `pnpm dev` uses: the
# session told line by line (HEARD, QUESTION, ANSWER, NO ANSWER), everything else dimmed.
# LOG_CONTENT=true writes the `content` field of an event (the words heard, prompts, model output). It is
# honoured at level trace, or in the story format outside production; everything else is always redacted.
# `pnpm dev` defaults to LOG_FORMAT=story, LOG_CONTENT=true, LOG_LEVEL=trace on this machine only, so the
# terminal shows the whole prompt and answer of every AI call (PROMPT, REPLY); LOG_LEVEL=debug hides them.
# Content that is allowed is written whole (not cut at 2,000 characters like every other string).
# NO_COLOR (any value) turns the story format's colours off.
# `pnpm dev` also writes what its servers print to .dev-local/dev.log (this run only, colours stripped).
# The servers then write to a pipe, so it sets FORCE_COLOR=1 to keep the terminal's colours unless
# NO_COLOR is set; a FORCE_COLOR of your own wins.
# LOG_LEVEL=info
# LOG_FORMAT=pretty
# LOG_CONTENT=false
# NO_COLOR=
